# Test: Looking glass searches an AS path typed with spaces, in the engine's grammar
# VALIDATES: against the real bgp-rib, with one peer holding 10.30.1.0/24
#            (AS path 65001 65002 65003), 10.30.2.0/24 (64999 65001 65002),
#            10.30.3.0/24 (65002 65001) and 10.30.4.0/24 (65001), the search
#            page (handleUISearch, internal/component/lg/handler_ui.go) asked
#            for "65001 65002" lists 10.30.1.0/24 and 10.30.2.0/24 and leaves
#            out the reverse order; asked for "^65001" it lists 10.30.1.0/24
#            and 10.30.4.0/24, the paths starting with 65001; asked for
#            "^65001 65002" it lists 10.30.1.0/24; asked for "65001$$" it
#            answers the error banner that names the value, the form the
#            field takes, and the example 65001 65002. One $ after the last
#            AS number is the end anchor bgp-rib reads, which
#            lg-origin-search.ci asks; the second $ is a character the engine
#            has no grammar for.
# PREVENTS: the page sending a spaced AS path as several tokens, which
#           bgp-rib refuses as an unknown keyword, and accepting characters
#           the engine cannot match
#           (spec-lg-search-matches-what-the-pages-promise AC-8, AC-9, AC-10).
#
# WHY A FUNCTIONAL TEST: the package tests feed bgp-rib's answers through a
# fake (internal/component/lg/ribfake_test.go), and the page's check
# (parseASPathPattern) cannot call the engine's validatePathPattern from the
# looking glass, which imports no RIB package. Only the real plugin proves
# that the comma form the page writes is the one matchASPath reads, and that
# the run is ordered and anchored as the pages describe.
#
# WHY A PLUGIN-DRIVEN HTTP CHECK (not an http= directive): the runner's http=
# check matches a substring, and the assertion is the set of rows each search
# lists, absences included. The native fixture
# (internal/test/fixture/lg_aspath_search.go) injects the routes, asks each
# question, and returns an error naming the rows it got when they differ.
# ZE_LG_PORT reaches the external fixture through the daemon's inherited
# environment (internal/component/plugin/process/process.go).

# ze-peer expectations (minimal handshake)
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=25s
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# Pass the looking-glass listen port ($PORT2) to the plugin process.
option=env:var=ZE_LG_PORT:value=$PORT2

# ze bgp configuration with LG enabled
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bus {
	endpoint bgp-rib {
		internal bgp-rib;
	}
	endpoint lg-aspath {
		external {
			run "le test fixture plugin/lg-aspath-search";
		}
		encoding json;
	}
}

environment {
	looking-glass {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
			# This test asserts looking-glass CONTENT over plain HTTP, so it takes
			# the documented TLS opt-out (spec-fixit-mgmt-listener-auth-guard AC-5).
			tls false
		}
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=20s
expect=exit:code=0
# The fixture prints this only after every search answered as expected; a
# failed one returns an error, which the runner reads as the test's failure.
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: AS path searches listed the routes of the run and refused the end anchor typed twice
